# Migrating `TimedExecutor` to .NET 8

## Key Breaking Changes

1. **`Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException`** in .NET 5+
2. **`Thread.ResetAbort()` is also removed**
3. Cooperative cancellation via `CancellationToken` is the modern pattern

## Migrated Code

```csharp
public class TimedExecutor
{
    public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<CancellationToken, T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);

        var task = Task.Run(() => work(cts.Token), cts.Token);

        try
        {
            return task.GetAwaiter().GetResult();
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException($"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
        catch (AggregateException ex) when (ex.InnerExceptions.Count == 1)
        {
            // Unwrap single exceptions for consistent behavior
            if (ex.InnerException is OperationCanceledException && cts.IsCancellationRequested)
                throw new TimeoutException($"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");

            throw new AggregateException(ex.InnerException!);
        }
    }

    // Overload preserving the original signature for non-cancellable work
    public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);

        var task = Task.Run(work, cts.Token);

        try
        {
            // Block until completion or timeout
            if (!task.Wait(timeout))
                throw new TimeoutException($"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");

            return task.Result;
        }
        catch (AggregateException ex) when (ex.InnerExceptions.Count == 1)
        {
            if (ex.InnerException is OperationCanceledException)
                throw new TimeoutException($"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");

            throw new AggregateException(ex.InnerException!);
        }
    }
}
```

## Critical Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Detail |
|---------|--------|
| **No forced termination** | Unlike `Thread.Abort()`, cancellation is *cooperative*. If `work` ignores the token, it keeps running after timeout. |
| **Thread pool starvation** | `Task.Run` uses the ThreadPool. Long-running non-cancellable work can starve other tasks. Use `TaskCreationOptions.LongRunning` if needed. |
| **`task.Result` double-wraps** | Accessing `.Result` wraps exceptions in `AggregateException`—handle unwrapping explicitly. |
| **Fire-and-forget leak** | The non-cancellable overload returns on timeout but the work continues in the background—this can cause resource leaks or race conditions. |

## If You Truly Need Hard Termination

For legacy code that can't adopt cooperative cancellation, isolate work in a **separate process**:

```csharp
// Last resort: out-of-process execution
var process = Process.Start(new ProcessStartInfo { /* ... */ });
if (!process.WaitForExit(timeout))
{
    process.Kill(entireProcessTree: true);
    throw new TimeoutException("...");
}
```

## Migration Checklist

1. Replace `Thread.Abort()`/`ResetAbort()` → `CancellationToken`
2. Refactor `work` lambdas to check `token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` in loops
3. Pass tokens through to I/O calls (`HttpClient`, `Stream.ReadAsync`, etc.)
4. Remove any `catch (ThreadAbortException)` blocks
5. Test timeout behavior—work no longer stops instantly
